+### The inner loop, and why a new worktree is not cold
+
+The flagless run above is the gate: everything, before a push. While you are
+still editing, two flags make the feedback much cheaper, and both are
+measurements of this repository rather than advice.
+
+```bash
+scripts/gates.sh --changed                 # fmt, clippy and the tests of the crates the diff touches
+scripts/gates.sh --changed --shared-target # the same, reusing the main checkout's build
+```
+
+`--changed` reads the diff (committed against the merge base with `main`,
+plus the working tree) and derives the packages from it: `crates/<dir>/…`
+resolves through that crate's manifest, so `crates/pixel` is `pixel-cli`.
+Clippy type-checks as it lints, so one scoped invocation replaces the
+edit/`cargo check` round trip, and the test step runs those crates' tests
+rather than the workspace's. It covers two things less than the gate, and
+says so when it finishes: the crates that *depend* on a changed library are
+compiled by the flagless run alone, and a change to a manifest, the lockfile
+or `.cargo/` widens the scope back to the whole workspace, because features
+and dependency versions unify there. Seventeen crates compile once per gate
+run; this is the loop that keeps an edit from paying for all of them.
+
+`--shared-target` points cargo at the main checkout's `target/` instead of
+this worktree's. Cargo keys a workspace member's fingerprint on its path
+*inside* the workspace, not on where the workspace lives — the dep-info
+records `src/lib.rs`, not an absolute path — so a worktree that has never
+built reuses the units the main checkout already has. Measured on this
+repository with `cargo check -p pixel-proto`, counting artifacts from cargo's
+JSON stream (the counts are the finding; the machine was busy, so read the
+seconds as indicative): a worktree that had never built recompiled 0 of 18
+units in 1 s against an existing build directory, and 18 of 18 in 21 s with a
+`target/` of its own. Two worktrees that diverge keep separate fingerprint
+slots — an edit in one recompiled exactly its own crate and left the other
+checkout's units fresh — so the flag does not make two branches fight over
+one directory. What it does cost is the directory's lock: a build already
+running in the main checkout makes the run wait for it (it queues, it does
+not fail), which is why it is a flag and not the default when several
+worktrees build at once. A `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` you set yourself always wins.
+
+Starting a worktree this way is the difference between a cold workspace build
+and a warm one; `just disk` and `just clean` still price and reclaim every
+worktree's output (see "Reclaiming disk"), and `clean.sh` leaves the
+per-worktree `target/` alone while `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` points elsewhere,
+because those directories are then not cargo's output.
